  <h1>Ice Memory</h1>
  <div class="story-meta">March 2026</div>
 </div>
 <div class="page-nav"><span class="page-info">Page 2 of 3</span><div class="page-links"><a href="/ice-memory.html?page=1#content" class="page-prev">&#8592; Previous</a><a href="/ice-memory.html?page=3#content" class="page-next">Next &#8594;</a></div></div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>Soren was there. He had been there for the first photograph and he was here for the second, and Markham had noticed that he had stopped making small talk, stopped asking her questions about her career and her research and the places she had been. He was watching her the way a person watches someone who is standing too close to the edge of something.</p>
<p>"Storm to break," he said. "In October. That's the beginning of winter on the sheet."</p>
<p>"Yes."</p>
<p>"They knew what they were getting into."</p>
<p>"Yes."</p>
<p>"But they were waiting for something."</p>
<p>Markham looked at the photograph again. The two men and the woman, their faces turned toward something she couldn't see. The camera held by the man on the left, pointed at the ground.</p>
<p>"They were waiting," she said.</p>
<p>By the end of the season — by which Markham meant the end of the working season, the six weeks in summer when the weather was just barely cooperative enough to keep the equipment running — they had found thirty-one photographs. They depicted twelve different people in various combinations: alone, in pairs, in groups of three or four. All of them on the ice sheet. All of them in clothing that dated to the 1920s. All of them in locations that corresponded to no known expedition.</p>
<p>She looked them all up, every name she could find, every reference to Greenland expeditions from that period. Most of the names didn't match anything in the historical record. A few did: a Norwegian cartographer who disappeared in 1925, a Danish surveyor whose last known communication was from 1928, a British geologist whose expedition was never recovered. None of them had records of photographs. None of them had explanations for what they were doing on the ice sheet in the locations where Markham's photographs showed them.</p>
<p>But the photographs showed them. Not just showed them — showed them waiting. Standing still, looking at things outside the frame, expressions that ranged from calm to urgent to something that Markham could only describe as <em>expectant</em>. They were waiting for something. All of them.</p>
<p>She brought the photographs back to Copenhagen and spent eighteen months trying to publish a paper. She failed. She failed in the way she had known she would fail, which was to say that the paper was rejected not on methodological grounds — the methodology was sound, the analysis was rigorous, the data was documented and verifiable — but on theoretical ones. The findings were impossible. The peer reviewers said so in language that ranged from skeptical to contemptuous.</p>
<p><em>This appears to be a hoax</em>, one reviewer wrote. <em>The methodological presentation is admirable, but the conclusions require a fundamental breakdown of physical law that I am not prepared to accept based on photographic evidence alone.</em></p>
<p><em>If we accept these findings</em>, another wrote, <em>we must accept that something is wrong with our understanding of ice sheet formation, or that the photographs were placed in the ice by some means we cannot identify. Neither option is acceptable.</em></p>
<p>Markham read the rejections and set them aside and did not cry, because she had learned a long time ago that crying was a luxury she couldn't afford. She had been denied tenure at two universities before she stopped applying for tenure-track positions. She had been called a fraud and a crank and, once, in a review of a book she had written about ice core analysis, "a well-meaning enthusiast with an overactive imagination." She knew what it felt like to be dismissed by people who hadn't looked carefully at what she was showing them. This was different. This was worse.</p>
<p>Because the photographs were real. The ice was real. The woman in the first photograph was real — had been real, in 1927 or whenever she had stood on the ice sheet with the camera pointed at her — and something had happened to her that no history recorded. Something had happened to all of them.</p>
<p>And Markham didn't know what it was. That was the part that kept her awake at night, lying in her narrow bed in her apartment in Copenhagen, listening to the sound of the city that never quite went quiet. She didn't know.</p>
<p>She left research in 2019. Took a teaching position at a small university in the American Midwest, in a town that was so flat and so featureless that the first time she arrived she had to check whether she had gotten off the plane in the right place. The university was the kind of institution that people who had never heard of it would never hear of. It had a good glaciology program, by the standards of institutions that still cared about glaciology, which was not as many as Markham would have liked.</p>
<p>She taught Introduction to Earth Systems and Advanced Topics in Paleoclimatology and a seminar she designed herself called The Ice Core Record, which attracted between six and twelve students per semester depending on the year and whether the university had managed to retain its funding. The students were mostly young and earnest and convinced that they wanted to save the planet. Markham liked them. She did not tell them about the photographs.</p>
<p>She told one person. Only one. Her name was Claire, and she was a graduate student who had come to Markham's office hours every week for a semester, asking questions about ice core data and paleoclimate proxies and the things that ice could reveal about the history of the atmosphere. Claire was twenty-seven and she had the kind of relentless curiosity that Markham recognized because she had been that person once, before the ice had taught her how much the world didn't want to be understood.</p>
<p>It was late in the semester, in December, and the snow was falling in the kind of heavy, wet flakes that Markham had always associated with the coast of Greenland rather than the American Midwest. Claire had come to office hours with a question about isotope analysis — a technical question, the kind that Markham would have been able to answer easily a decade ago and that now required her to think carefully and write things down before she was sure of the answer. They talked for an hour. When the conversation turned to something else — to the nature of evidence, to the question of what it meant to accept that something was true — Markham found herself telling Claire about the photographs.</p>
<p>She told her all of it. The depth. The age of the ice. The paper stock and the image style and the names on the backs of the photographs. The rejections. The fact that she had left research because she couldn't find anyone who would take the findings seriously, and she had given up trying to make them listen.</p>
<p>Claire listened without interrupting. When Markham finished, Claire was quiet for a long time. The snow was still falling outside the window, and the light in the office was the gray-white of winter afternoons in the Midwest, flat and even and somehow heavier than other light.</p>
<p>"What do you think happened to them?" Claire asked.</p>
<p>Markham shook her head. "I don't know."</p>
<p>"But you have a theory."</p>
<p>"I have a hypothesis. It's not scientific. I can't test it."</p>
<p>"Tell me anyway."</p>
<p>Markham looked at the window. The snow was accumulating on the sill, building up in a way that was familiar — the way snow built up on surfaces that didn't get disturbed, that were allowed to accumulate without interference. She had seen that building-up on the ice sheet, in places where the wind didn't blow hard enough to clear it away.</p>
<p>"Ice is not just frozen water," she said. "That's what we tell students. We simplify. We say ice is frozen water and ice cores give us data about temperature and atmospheric composition. But ice is more complicated than that. Ice forms under pressure. Ice changes over time. The ice at the bottom of a deep core is not the same as the ice at the surface. It's been compressed, transformed, subjected to conditions that we don't fully understand."</p>
<p>"You're saying the ice might have properties we haven't accounted for."</p>
<p>"I'm saying ice might remember things."</p>
<p>Claire frowned. "Remember how?"</p>
<p>Markham turned from the window. "When you drill a core and pull it up, you're not just extracting data. You're extracting a record. The ice has been recording everything that happened around it — every snowfall, every chemical change in the atmosphere, every shift in temperature. It records. It doesn't just store information passively. It <em>records</em>. Which means it's doing something active. Something we don't understand."</p>
<p>"You're saying the ice recorded the photographs."</p>
<p>"I'm saying the ice recorded something. The photographs are evidence of that recording. But I don't know what the ice was recording, or why, or how it knew to record that specific thing at that specific time."</p>
